scenario_questions.scenario:
A product page shows a spinner while computing a price. Users report that the spinner often never appears, but the logs show the code runs. You have this handler:

```typescript
async function onRecalculate() {
  spinner.classList.add('visible'); // CSS shows spinner when .visible is set
  await Promise.resolve(); // price is fast when cached (simulated here)
  spinner.classList.remove('visible');
}

button.addEventListener('click', onRecalculate);
```

Relevant CSS:

```css
.spinner { opacity: 0; transition: opacity 150ms; }
.spinner.visible { opacity: 1; }
```

On fast paths the spinner doesn't appear at all. On slow paths (network), it does. You must decide how to guarantee at least one paint of the spinner without stalling the app.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
The class is added and removed within the same task/microtask barrier. Browsers batch style recalculation and painting; microtasks (like the awaited already-resolved promise) run before yielding to rendering, so the DOM changes happen back-to-back without a frame in between. No paint occurs with the spinner visible, so users never see it.

A fix is to yield to the rendering pipeline after adding the class, scheduling the work for a future frame or task. For example, wait for the next animation frame or a macrotask tick before starting the async work or removing the class:

```typescript
async function onRecalculate() {
  spinner.classList.add('visible');
  await new Promise(requestAnimationFrame); // yield to next frame so paint occurs
  const price = await computePrice(); // actual work
  spinner.classList.remove('visible');
}
```

Alternatively, split the fast-path await out of the first turn of the event loop with `await Promise.resolve()` before adding the class, or use `setTimeout(0)` to defer removal:

```typescript
async function onRecalculate() {
  spinner.classList.add('visible');
  await new Promise(res => setTimeout(res, 0)); // macrotask boundary; allows paint
  const price = await computePrice();
  spinner.classList.remove('visible');
}
```

Trade-offs: `requestAnimationFrame` aligns with rendering and avoids unnecessary extra delay; `setTimeout(0)` yields to the task queue but may introduce ~4ms clamping on background tabs. Both ensure at least one paint without arbitrarily delaying beyond one frame.

scenario_questions.scenario:
You are designing a small TypeScript utility to fetch JSON and parse it into a typed value. Current code:

```typescript
async function getJson(url: string, parse: (j: any) => any) {
  const res = await fetch(url);
  const json = await res.json();
  return parse(json);
}

// Usage:
const user = await getJson('/api/user', j => ({ id: j.id, name: j.name }));
```

Problems observed: Callers can accidentally return a wrong shape undetected; and errors in `parse` throw as `any`, losing type information.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Define a generic over T and type the parse parameter as either a mapper `(j: unknown) => T` or a predicate `(j: unknown) => j is T`. Use overloads to give callers a precise return type. Avoid `any` and surface a typed error for validation.

```typescript
type ParseError = { kind: 'ParseError'; message: string };

export function getJson<T>(url: string, parse: (j: unknown) => j is T): Promise<T>;
export function getJson<T>(url: string, parse: (j: unknown) => T): Promise<T>;
export async function getJson<T>(url: string, parse: ((j: unknown) => T) | ((j: unknown) => j is T)) {
  const res = await fetch(url);
  const json: unknown = await res.json();
  if (isPredicate(parse)) {
    if (parse(json)) return json as T;
    throw { kind: 'ParseError', message: 'Validation failed' } as ParseError;
  } else {
    return (parse as (j: unknown) => T)(json);
  }
}

function isPredicate<T>(fn: any): fn is (j: unknown) => j is T {
  return fn.length === 1 && /=>\s*j\s+is/.test(String(fn)) === false; // simplified; in practice, choose a different strategy
}

// Usage 1: mapper
const user = await getJson('/api/user', (j: unknown) => {
  const o = j as { id: number; name: string };
  if (typeof o.id !== 'number' || typeof o.name !== 'string') throw { kind: 'ParseError', message: 'Bad user' } as ParseError;
  return { id: o.id, name: o.name };
});

// Usage 2: predicate
function isUser(j: unknown): j is { id: number; name: string } {
  return !!j && typeof (j as any).id === 'number' && typeof (j as any).name === 'string';
}
const user2 = await getJson('/api/user', isUser);
```

Justification: `unknown` forces callers to prove shapes via parsing or predicates, preventing accidental `any` leaks. Generics ensure the returned `Promise<T>` reflects the parser. Predicate overload preserves T when returning the original JSON on success. A dedicated `ParseError` type can be caught and distinguished from network errors.

Root cause: Flex items have `min-width:auto`, which equals the min-content size, so the `.main` cannot shrink below its longest unbreakable line (code), forcing overflow.

Fix: Allow flex items to shrink by resetting min-width and bias width via flex factors at narrow widths.

```css
.layout { display: flex; }
.main, .side { flex: 1 1 0; min-width: 0; }
@media (max-width: 600px) {
  .main { flex: 2 1 0; } /* main gets more space */
  .side { flex: 1 1 0; }
}
/* Optional to preserve content without clipping: allow scrolling within main if still needed */
.main { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
```

`min-width: 0` lets items shrink below their min-content size, preventing page-level overflow. `flex-basis: 0` with adjusted flex-grow biases space toward `.main` on small screens while keeping a single row when there's room. Verification: Resize to 320px (no horizontal scrollbar; main dominates width), 768px (both readable, still one row), 1024px+ (even distribution unless styles adjust). Use DevTools to check no overflow on body and that `.main`'s content wraps or scrolls locally if truly unbreakable.

